<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarnovo Constitution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> Constitution was the first constitution of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, adopted by a constituent assembly at Veliko Tarnovo on 16 April 1879. One of the most liberal of its time, it set up a single-chamber National Assembly, guaranteed civil rights and limited the powers of the prince. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-battenberg">Alexander of Battenberg</a> suspended it in 1881, but it was restored in 1883 and remained in force until 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarnovo-constitution.jpg" alt="Tarnovo Constitution" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bulgarian Constituent Assembly · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BASA-173K-6-3116-Tarnovo_Constitution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The constitution was adopted by the Constituent National Assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Veliko Tarnovo</a> as part of the establishment of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. It was based on the Belgian charter of 1831 and was considered advanced for its time. Because the Treaty of Berlin of 1878 restricted Bulgaria's independence and made it a de facto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, the monarch bore the title of Prince rather than tsar, as it had been during the First and the Second Bulgarian Empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The constitution made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> a constitutional, hereditary monarchy with a parliament elected by the people, and divided power between executive, legislative and judicial branches. The prince had to be male and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, although a legal exception allowed the election of the Lutheran Alexander of Battenberg. Ministers took responsibility for the acts they signed, deputies had immunity, and the prince's signature was needed for a bill to become law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a> was the official religion, but followers of other faiths were held equal. Aristocratic titles and censorship were banned, property was declared sacrosanct, all male citizens, the monarch included, had to serve in the army, and citizens could form parties and companies. Article 61 freed any slave who set foot on Bulgarian territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The constitution was often set aside. The ban on censorship was suspended in the 1880s by a law of the Russian general Sobolev, which restricted the press to seven newspapers and magazines, and the whole constitution was suspended in 1881 by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-national-assembly-of-turkey">Grand National Assembly</a> that Alexander manipulated. Amendments in 1893 and 1911 strengthened royal power, and after Prince Ferdinand proclaimed independence in 1908 the text was amended to name the country the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and to replace Prince with tzar.</p><p>It was suspended again after the coups of 1934, led by Kimon Georgiev, and of 1944, organised by the Bulgarian Communist Party; in 1945 the People's Tribunal, which had not the status of a court, passed more than 10,000 sentences. The referendum of 1946, held under Soviet occupation, ended the monarchy, though the constitution made no provision for such a change, and on 4 December 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimitrov-constitution">Dimitrov Constitution</a> replaced it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tarnovo Constitution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Bulgaria the memory of the Asen tsars sustained a sense of nationhood under Ottoman rule; Paisius of Hilendar celebrated them in his Slavonic-Bulgarian History of 1762, and when a Bulgarian state was restored in 1878, Tarnovo hosted the assembly that adopted the <b>Tarnovo Constitution</b> of 1879.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1879 the <b>Tarnovo Constitution</b> was adopted and Alexander of Battenberg elected prince, with Sofia as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bulgaria">Principality of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
